# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for RustOwl.
#
# Referenced by scripts/security.sh (run_thread_sanitizer_tests) via
# TSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=.tsan-suppressions.
#
# Each entry must state WHY the pattern is suppressed, and what would make it
# safe to remove. Silently listing a frame defeats the purpose of running a race
# detector at all.

# Not real races, and not the same artefact as the one tokio's own suite hits.
#
# There are two distinct false-positive shapes here, and only one of them is
# fixed by -Zbuild-std:
#
# 1. The init-then-read shape, where the happens-before edge lives inside
#    `Once`/`OnceLock` or a `Mutex` in std. Building against an uninstrumented
#    std hides that edge, so TSan reports e.g.
#       Once::call_once_force
#         -> OnceLock<tokio::signal::registry::Globals>>::initialize
#         -> tokio::runtime::signal::Driver::new
#    -Zbuild-std instruments std and this disappears. RustOwl does not produce
#    it: zero of these have ever been observed here. tokio's own suite is clean
#    under -Zbuild-std on aarch64-apple-darwin (136 tests),
#    aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu and x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu (139 each), and with
#    --all-features plus --cfg tokio_unstable (167) -- the --all-features build
#    needs tokio_unstable or it will not compile.
#    https://github.com/samuraixwandering/tokio/actions/runs/30937545182
#
# 2. The reactor registration shape, which is what we actually hit:
#       Previous write: RegistrationSet::allocate -> Box::new(ArcInner<ScheduledIo>)
#       Atomic read:    ScheduledIo::set_readiness            (reactor worker)
#    The registration is published to the reactor through epoll, so the
#    synchronisation is a syscall TSAN cannot instrument. -Zbuild-std does NOT
#    remove this: verified locally, an instrumented build with -Zbuild-std still
#    reports exactly these two. tokio's own test suite never opens a TCP
#    connection through the reactor, which is why it stays clean.
#
# No RustOwl code appears in any racing access.
#
# Remove once tokio either annotates the reactor handoff for TSAN or ships its
# own suppression list. A new entry should only be added with the same level of
# evidence -- a deliberate race first detected in a known-good -Zbuild-std build,
# not "we saw a warning".
race:tokio::runtime::io::scheduled_io
race:tokio::runtime::io::registration_set